ound the world have been making moves  to return artifacts and remains to First  nations in Canada this week members of  the niska First Nation visited the  national museum of Scotland to begin the  process of returning a stolen totem pole  Professor Dan Hicks says all museums  need to look closer at their collections  we can't be you know treating one form  of theft in in the rightful way while  hiding away these other issues in the  storerooms OR refusing to speak about  them after a century degrees of theft  the process to return artifacts of  living cultures to their rightful homes  is one that is going to take decades  Redmond Shannon Global News London  Hotel Heist how did this Priceless  portrait in Ottawa get swiped under  everyone's noses  now to the mystery and investigation  into an art Heist at the Chateau Laurier  in Ottawa this photograph of Sir Winston  Churchill hanging in the hotel's reading  Lounge since 1998 was stolen and swapped  out with a fake about eight months ago  the original one is one of the most  famous portraits of the former British  prime minister and as Dan Spector  explains the copy was so similar nobody  noticed the fame piece was missing until  now  ottawa's storied Chateau Laurier hotel  needs your help to solve a Hollywood  caliber Caper that's captivating art  lovers the world over for us it means  the world since 1998 the hotel's reading  room was home to a unique print of  world-renowned Armenian Canadian  photographer Yusuf karsh's famous  roaring lion picture of former British  prime minister Winston Churchill but  late last week a maintenance worker  noticed it was hanging by a wire instead  of anchors so then we knew it was  probably a copy it was confirmed this  Saturday by The Cars estate longtime  friend and Associate of the late artist  Jerry Fielder took one look and  confirmed the original version gifted to  the hotel by karsh himself had been  swapped out for a fake nobody noticed  for over eight months I think he would  be shocked and amazed and disappointed  that someone would do this  in 1941 as the second world war raged on  European soil Churchill visited Ottawa  delivering a stirring address to the  House of Commons the fight on in unity  together  it was after that speech that the  celebrated photographer captured the  iconic photo that even appears on the  British five pound note the legendary  scowl came after karsh took the cigar  out of Churchill's mouth it became what  we would say today viral this expert in  recovering stolen art says because karsh  lived and worked at the hotel for 20  years the portrait's value is likely in  the six-figure range it's quite possible  that a Insider was involved the hotel  has now removed other famous works as it  re-evaluates security authorities  believe the swap took place between  December 25th and January 6th the hope  is the public can help figure out  exactly when it happened by sharing  photos they may have taken with the  portrait in the background I think  somehow we're going to  find who did it any clues any earring of  somebody trying to sell that portrait we  would love to know karsh's friend says  police have been doing an excellent job  Dan Specter Global News Montreal  out of a movie that's global national  for this Wednesday night I'm Farah  Nasser on behalf of our whole team here  at global national I want to thank you  so much for spending part of your  evening with us tonight's your Canada is  Burdette Alberta we love seeing your  Canada so please keep emailing us those  photos to viewers at globalnational.com  until tomorrow take care of yourselves  and each other good night  foreign
 

